        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Egypt &amp; North Africa</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Second Battle of El Alamein</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">23 October – 4 November 1942 (a 13-day battle; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> retreat and the Allied pursuit continued to 11 November)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Near the railway halt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-alamein">El Alamein</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, on a front between the sea and the Qattara Depression, about 50 miles (80 km) from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The British Commonwealth Eighth Army under Lieutenant-General Bernard Montgomery, with 195,000 men and 1,029 tanks; the German-Italian Panzer Army (Panzerarmee Afrika) under Field Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erwin-rommel">Erwin Rommel</a>, with 116,000 men and 547 tanks</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> defences were broken and Panzerarmee Afrika was forced to retreat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>; most of the Italian infantry, which lacked transport, was abandoned. The victory ended the Axis threat to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</span></div>
        </div>
