    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Montgomery planned a battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a> in which his superiority in men, tanks, guns and aircraft would 'crumble' the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> defences; he expected it to last 12 days, and it lasted 13.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> fuel was short throughout: by 25 October Panzerarmee Afrika had three days' supply, only two days' of it east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobruk">Tobruk</a>, and tankers carrying fuel were sunk at the end of October and in early November.</li><li>Casualty figures differ by source: Eighth Army lost 13,560 men, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> losses are put at 36,939 in one reckoning that Niall Barr calls very uncertain, and as much larger totals once the retreat is counted.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Before the attack Eighth Army hid its build-up in the north with dummy dumps under camouflage nets, dummy tanks and guns of calico and palm-frond hurdles in the south, real tanks disguised as trucks with plywood superstructures, and a dummy water pipeline built slowly enough to suggest it would not be finished until after the attack.</p></aside>
    </section>

