    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">May 1942</span><span class="rf-text">Panzer Army <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> wins the Battle of Gazala and advances into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1942</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> attacks in the First Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-alamein">El Alamein</a> are defeated</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1942</span><span class="rf-text">Churchill and Brooke replace Auchinleck with Alexander, and Montgomery takes command of Eighth Army</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Late August 1942</span><span class="rf-text">Rommel's attack is repulsed at the Alam el Halfa ridge</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1942</span><span class="rf-text">Operation Lightfoot opens with a barrage of 1,000 guns</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">November 1942</span><span class="rf-text">Operation Supercharge begins, and the 9th Armoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a> attacks the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> gun line; on 4 November the Axis front is broken and Rommel orders a retreat</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">January 1943</span><span class="rf-text">Eighth Army takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> as Rommel withdraws to the Mareth Line</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="second-battle-of-el-alamein" data-v="45f21e18d9" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The armies stand in their lines</b> The Eighth Army stood in the east with XXX Corps in the north, XIII Corps in the south and X Corps ready to exploit. Rommel split his armour into a northern and a southern group, held the 90th Light Division further back and kept the Trieste Division in reserve near the coast.</li><li><b>The break-in: infantry advance, armour is held up</b> After a barrage, the four infantry divisions of XXX Corps advanced into the Axis minefields and the armour crawled up behind. Only about half of the infantry reached their objectives and none of the tanks broke through. In the south XIII Corps met determined resistance.</li><li><b>Axis tanks counter-attack and the Australians strike north</b> At dusk on 24 October tanks of the northern group advanced from the Kidney feature against the 1st Armoured Division. Over 100 tanks fought and half were destroyed by dark, with neither position altered. The Australians attacked Point 29 at midnight on 25 October.</li><li><b>Rommel brings his armour north</b> Rommel reversed his policy of spreading his armour along the front. The 90th Light Division came forward and the 21st Panzer Division went north with a third of the Ariete Division. A shortage of fuel meant the move could not be reversed.</li><li><b>Operation Supercharge and the charge of the 9th Armoured</b> Two assault brigades cleared a path from 01:05 and gained most of their objectives. At 06:15 the 9th Armoured Brigade advanced on the Axis gun line and failed to create the gap, though it caused great damage.</li><li><b>The Axis tanks counter-attack and fail</b> The remains of the 9th Armoured Brigade came under the 1st Armoured Division, which had deployed. The northern and southern armoured groups counter-attacked at 11:00 and failed under shells and bombs at a cost of about 100 tanks.</li><li><b>The front is broken and Rommel orders a retreat</b> British armour burst a 19-kilometre hole in the Axis front. Rommel could wait no longer for Hitler's reply and at 17:30 gave the order to retreat. The two armoured groups pulled back to the west.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/winston-churchill.jpg" alt="Winston Churchill" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Winston_Churchill_-_19086236948_(restored).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After its success at Gazala, Panzer Army <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, made up of German and Italian units under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erwin-rommel">Erwin Rommel</a>, advanced into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and threatened British control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and its oil resources. General Claude Auchinleck withdrew Eighth Army to within 50 miles (80 km) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-alamein">El Alamein</a> line, where the Qattara Depression, 40 miles (64 km) to the south, was impassable and any attack had to be frontal. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> attacks in the First Battle of El Alamein (1–27 July) were defeated, and Eighth Army counter-attacks in July also failed.</p><p>In early August <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> and General Sir Alan Brooke visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and replaced Auchinleck as Commander-in-Chief Middle East Command with General Harold Alexander. Lieutenant-General William Gott, made commander of Eighth Army, was killed when his transport aircraft was shot down, and Lieutenant-General Bernard Montgomery was flown from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to replace him. Rommel, lacking reinforcements, dependent on small ports for supplies and aware of a huge Allied reinforcement, attacked first, but was repulsed at the Alam el Halfa ridge on 30 August 1942.</p><p>The short front and secure flanks favoured the defence, and Rommel used the time to sow minefields of about 500,000 mines, with miles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbed-wire">barbed wire</a>. Alexander and Montgomery intended to build a superiority of force sufficient for a breakthrough that could be exploited to destroy Panzerarmee Afrika. Earlier in the campaign neither side had been able to exploit a local victory enough to defeat its opponent before it withdrew.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After six more weeks of preparation Eighth Army was ready, with 195,000 men and 1,029 tanks against the 116,000 men and 547 tanks of the Panzerarmee. It drew men and materials from the United Kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, and Sherman tanks from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Montgomery's plan, Operation Lightfoot, was a main attack in the north by XXX Corps (Lieutenant-General Oliver Leese) and a secondary attack in the south by XIII Corps (Lieutenant-General Brian Horrocks), while X Corps (Lieutenant-General Herbert Lumsden) was to exploit the success. He expected a 12-day battle in three stages: the break-in, the dogfight and the final breaking of the enemy.</p><p>Under Operation Bertram the British deceived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> about where and when the attack would fall. Rommel was ill and left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on 23 September, and General der Panzertruppe Georg Stumme took his place. The defences were laid out in at least two belts of mines about 3.1 miles (5 km) apart, linked into boxes known as Devil's gardens, and the forward lines alternated German and Italian infantry formations.</p><p>Rommel did not want the British armour to reach open ground, since he had neither the numbers nor the fuel for a battle of manoeuvre. Because the deception left him unsure where the blow would come, he split his armour into a northern group (15th Panzer Division and the Littorio Division) and a southern group (21st Panzer Division and the Ariete Division). The 15th Panzer Division had 125 operational tanks and the 21st Panzer Division 121.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At 21:40 on 23 October, Operation Lightfoot began with a 1,000-gun barrage, which went on for five and a half hours and used about 529,000 shells. The four infantry divisions of XXX Corps advanced from 22:00, but the minefields were deeper than expected, clearing them was slow, and the tanks raised so much dust and jammed so badly that none broke through. Only about half of the infantry reached their objectives. On 24 October Stumme suffered a heart attack and died, and Major-General Wilhelm Ritter von Thoma took temporary command. At dusk on the 24th the first big tank engagement began at the Kidney feature, with over 100 tanks involved; half were destroyed by dark.</p><p>Montgomery shifted his main effort north with the 9th Australian Division, while Rommel ordered the 90th Light Division and the 21st Panzer Division north, a move that fuel shortage made impossible to reverse. On 27 October at Snipe the Rifle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a>, with thirteen 6-pounder anti-tank guns and six more, held off tank attacks and destroyed 22 German and 10 Italian tanks. On 28 October the 15th and 21st Panzer Divisions attacked again but were halted, Rommel's last attempt to take the initiative.</p><p>Operation Supercharge began at 01:00 on 2 November with a barrage of 360 guns firing 15,000 shells. The 9th Armoured Brigade attacked at 06:15 with 94 runners and was reduced to 24, and 230 of the 400 men involved were killed, wounded or captured; it did not open the gap, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> counter-attack at 11:00 failed at a cost of about 100 tanks. On 3 November Hitler ordered Rommel to stand fast. On 4 November the Axis front was broken and at 17:30 Rommel ordered a retreat.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Second Battle of El Alamein&#8221; cites where it covers Phase one: The Break-In, Phase two: The Crumbling, Phase three: the counter, Phase four: Operation Supercharge:</p><ul><li>Cassandra Vivian, The Western Desert of Egypt: An Explorer's Handbook (American University in Cairo Press, 2000)</li><li>Jon Latimer, Alamein (John Murray, 2002)</li><li>Paolo Caccia Dominioni de Sillavengo, Alamein 1933–1962: An Italian Story (Allen &amp; Unwin, 1966)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Because of lack of transport most of the Italian infantry formations were abandoned, since Hitler's insistence that Rommel hold his ground had obliged him to keep the non-motorised Italian units forward until it was too late. The Ariete Armoured Division was destroyed in a determined resistance on 4 November, and the Littorio and Trieste Divisions were also destroyed. The Bologna Division and the remnants of the Trento Division marched into the desert without water, food or transport before surrendering, exhausted and dying from dehydration. By 11 November <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> prisoners numbered 30,000.</p><p>Eighth Army was surprised by the withdrawal and was slow in pursuit, failing to cut off Rommel at Fuka and Mersa Matruh. The 1st Armoured Division ran out of fuel, rain on 6 November bogged down a fuel convoy, and waterlogged ground and lack of fuel stranded two armoured divisions on 7 November. On 11 November the 5th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> Infantry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a> captured the Halfaya Pass, taking 600 Italian prisoners, and the Egyptian frontier was clear.</p><p>Eighth Army had 13,560 casualties: 2,350 killed, 8,950 wounded and 2,260 missing. It lost from 332 to 500 tanks, 300 of which had been repaired by the end of the battle. British figures based on Ultra give German casualties as 1,149 killed, 3,886 wounded and 8,050 captured, and Italian losses as 971 dead, 933 wounded and 15,552 captured. About 500 Axis tanks were lost. The Axis then withdrew by stages through El Agheila to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>, which fell on 23 January 1943, and on to the Mareth Line in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini.jpg" alt="Benito Mussolini" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mussolini_mezzobusto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Much of what is known of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> side comes from Rommel's own signals and diary, quoted in the histories, and from Fritz Bayerlein's notes to The Rommel Papers. For the British, Ultra decrypts and local sources exposed the Axis order of battle, supply position and intentions, so that supply ships could be found and attacked. Until the end of June 1942 Rommel had in turn read detailed reports on British forces sent to Washington by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> military attaché in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, Colonel Bonner Fellers, because the American code had been stolen. Lucas-Phillips's Alamein and the regimental histories give the view from the units that fought.</p><p>The casualty figures vary between sources. Niall Barr wrote in 2005 that the figure of 36,939 for Panzerarmee casualties was very uncertain because of the chaos of the Axis retreat. Bayerlein estimated German losses at 1,100 killed, 3,900 wounded and 7,900 prisoners and Italian losses at 1,200 killed, 1,600 wounded and 20,000 prisoners. The Italian official history gives Axis losses in the battle as 4,000 to 5,000 killed or missing, 7,000 to 8,000 wounded and 17,000 prisoners, rising during the retreat to 9,000 killed or missing, 15,000 wounded and 35,000 prisoners.</p><p>Historians debate why Rommel advanced into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. In 1997 Martin van Creveld wrote that German and Italian staffs had advised that his army could not be supplied so far from Tripoli and Benghazi. According to Maurice Remy (2002), Hitler and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> pressed him to advance in order to take the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> and the Middle Eastern oil fields.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Allied victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-alamein">El Alamein</a> was the beginning of the end of the Western Desert Campaign. It ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> threat to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, and revived the morale of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> as their first large success against the Axis since Operation Crusader in late 1941. Its end coincided with the Allied landing in French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-torch">Operation Torch</a> on 8 November. Italian and German forces in North Africa capitulated in May 1943.</p><p>Alexander suggested to Churchill that he ring out the bells. Speaking at the Lord Mayor's Banquet on 10 November 1942, Churchill emphasised the victory's importance but warned against premature celebration: 'This is not the end. It is not even the beginning of the end. But it is perhaps the end of the beginning.' In his memoirs in 1950 he wrote that 'Before Alamein we never had a victory. After Alamein we never had a defeat'.</p><p>The battle is remembered for episodes within it. The regimental historian of the Rifle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a> called the action at Snipe the most famous day of the regiment's war, and its commanding officer, Lieutenant-Colonel Victor Buller Turner, was awarded the Victoria Cross. Rommel wrote that the Italians of the Ariete fought with exemplary courage. Montgomery had foreseen an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a> battle and accurately predicted its length and the number of British and Commonwealth casualties.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-alamein">El Alamein</a> in October and November 1942 Montgomery's Eighth Army, with 195,000 men and 1,029 tanks, broke the fortified line held by Rommel's 116,000 men and 547 tanks after 13 days of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> army retreated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> to the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>.</em></p>

